Performance
===========

- Resolved: descriptor spec was re-validated on every module call — validate_descriptor →
  validate_descriptor_spec (src/main.rs) deep-copied each spec field (spec struct, then
  documentation/arity/type/return, including full copies of the type and return arrays) per
  check per call. validate_descriptor_spec now borrows the cells in place (field_cell /
  spec_borrow, no copy()); every check and error message is unchanged, and the spec is still
  read live at each call, so `set` into a bound module's descriptor takes effect immediately
  (locked by a test in src/tests/modules.rs). Benchmark (release, 100k calls to str.upper in a
  loop, best of 5): 250 ms → 178 ms — the module-call tax dropped ~40% (~0.7 µs/call), with
  plain-fn and bare-loop baselines unchanged. Residual: an (arity, type) identity cache would
  remove the remaining scans, but it would go stale if `set` mutates a spec — that belongs
  with the persistent-representation work below. (was 10)

- Resolved: deep copy on every read — the core cost of the "value semantics with explicit ^
  aliasing" design. Every variable read, field access, and index did a full recursive copy() of
  arrays/structs, and every set/bind ran a full O(n) graph walk with fresh HashSets for cycle
  detection. Model P replaces this: array/struct snapshots are immutable (Rc<Vec<Value>> /
  Rc<Vec<(String, Value)>>), so reads are O(1)-shallow (shared) clones; a ^ alias is a logical
  location (root Cell + path, resolved against the current value at read/write time — it never
  pins an intermediate node, so a whole-value rebind invalidates element/field aliases and they
  fail deterministically instead of exposing stale data); writes resolve the terminal location
  (follow cell chains + translate intermediate value aliases: write-through), rebuild the
  immutable snapshot chain along the path (whole-array/struct copy per level, O(children)), and
  replace the value in the terminal cell — aliases stay live while rebinds stay isolated. Cycle
  detection walks only when the written value contains a Ref (all 8 locked cases still say
  "cyclic reference"; sibling/sibling and helper/root chains now caught via the landing-walk).
  Benchmark (release, 100k iters, best-of-3, ns/iter; before → after A1–A3 → Model P): bare
  646→565→627, fn_int 974→891→1002, fn_arr5 1431→1350→1129, fn_arr100 7385→7295→2411 (3.0×),
  fn_nested5x5 3662→3566→1712 (2.1×), index_read 1154→992→949, field_read 1044→965→1036,
  nested_read 1148→917→1103, set_elem_scalar 928→664→906, set_nested 1044→671→1040,
  set_elem_big 10607→4521→2521 (4.2× vs before). The deep-copy tax on array-heavy work is gone
  (≈3× on fn_arr100, ≈2× on fn_nested5x5); small-scalar cases jitter ±10–60 ns from allocator
  noise. Residual: each set rebuilds the full array/struct chain at every level (O(children)
  per level) — a structural-sharing/persistent-Vec (e.g. a 32-way trie) would make array writes
  sub-linear; and intermediate alias chains make field/nested reads a hair slower than the old
  flat-cell layout. (was 5)

  The Model P semantics were locked by a 10-area audit (13 tests in src/tests/semantics.rs,
  caeef52): RefLocation holds only (root Cell + path), root replacement invalidates incompatible
  paths deterministically, reads/binds/args are value snapshots, aliases write through with
  replace-at-leaf at final positions, cycles still fail with "cyclic reference", and %v keeps
  the Ref(...) wrapper while %s/%x/%j/%q deref. The 32-way persistent vector remains a SEPARATE
  future optimization, deliberately not part of this change; re-evaluate only if array-write
  benchmarks dominate real workloads (see the decision note in the Model P summary).

Bug
===

- Resolved: no tail calls and no recursion-depth guard — deep or infinite recursion used to
  overflow the native stack and abort (panic = "abort") with no diagnostic. The evaluator now
  bounds call depth: MAX_CALL_DEPTH (2048) nested calls; past that, `RecursionError: call depth
  limit (2048) exceeded …` points at the offending call (file:line:col + caret) and carries the
  live call chain (innermost 40 frames, then "… N more frame(s) omitted"). The interpreter runs
  on a thread with a large explicit stack (256 MB, main()) so the guard — not the native stack —
  is what bounds recursion; 2048 was calibrated against measured frame costs (~28–44 KB per call
  level in debug builds, ~6 KB in release; the tree-walker is stack-hungry). Recursion near the
  limit still works; `loop` remains the unbounded iteration form. Residual: `(eval …)`-driven
  self-nesting, deeply nested data (copy/render), and deep parser nesting have their own native
  recursion the call guard does not count. (was 6)

- Open: tail-call optimization — tail-recursive functions (and their calls inside if/match/
  block tails) would run in constant stack, making tail recursion unbounded and widening the
  usable recursion headroom far beyond the guard. The guard above means this is no longer a
  crash hazard; it is a performance/expressiveness item. (from the item above)

- Global mutable state duplicated as bookkeeping. Five thread_local! slots including LAST_ERROR_SPAN, which is written on every eval node (src/main.rs:811). That's thread-local borrow traffic on the hot path, and the "last span any node touched" scheme is fragile — an error in a native function points at whatever node happened to evaluate last, not necessarily the failing call. (was 8)

Feature
=======

- use special-cases the AST shape (let …) instead of evaluating normally. (from 10)

- Module descriptor spec.type has a design hole: each argument maps to exactly one type name, which cannot express polymorphic arguments. The `"any"` marker (added for the http module body, src/modules/http.rs) is a stopgap that matches any value type but is not part of the core type vocabulary — decide how to model it properly (e.g. type unions, a first-class Any, or per-arg validator functions).
